  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 210 PM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThis drier pattern begins to give way to better moisture this \nweekend. The center of the high pressure shifts further east, \nsliding slowly across NM and allowing moisture to gradually increase \nunderneath in and on its western periphery. Precipitation on \nSaturday likely remains confined to the high terrain across our \narea, especially across our southwest mountains. The high shifts \nfurther eastward on Sunday, aided by a sharp trough pushing its way \ninto the Pacific Northwest. This looks to create another convergent \nflow of moisture across AZ and NM going into Sunday and early next \nweek. With that, precipitation chances greatly increase across \nour area, specifically the central mountain chain westward. This \nfavors to be the start of an active wet pattern going through at \nleast the middle of next week. Through each day, scattered to \nnumerous are favored across most of the area, especially western \nNM. Deterministic models differ on whether we can get additional \nforcing from speed maxes moving through the trough (which could \nincrease coverage and intensity of precipitation at times). They \nalso differ on if a backdoor front or general moisture boundary \nmoves into NM during the early to middle part of next week. This \nwould increase moisture especially across eastern NM. Main \nmessages with the long term period is that a favorable pattern for\nmore widespread precipitation is forecast starting Sunday and \ninto next week, though details for each day remain fuzzy \ncurrently.",
